(i) The L.S. of a maize grain is drawn as an oval grain in which a large endosperm fills most of the upper part and the embryo lies to one side at the lower end. Any six of these labels:
- **seed coat and fruit wall (pericarp)**, fused together as the outer covering;
- **aleurone layer**, the protein-rich outer layer of the endosperm;
- **endosperm**, the large food-storing tissue;
- **scutellum**, the single large shield-shaped cotyledon lying against the endosperm;
- **coleoptile**, the hollow foliar sheath enclosing the shoot apex;
- **plumule** (shoot apex with leaf primordia);
- **epiblast**;
- **radicle** with the **root cap**;
- **coleorhiza**, the undifferentiated sheath enclosing the radicle and root cap.

(ii) One difference:

| Perisperm | Endosperm |
|---|---|
| The residual, persistent nucellus in the seed | Formed from the primary endosperm cell after double fertilisation |
| Diploid (2n) | Triploid (3n) |

Examples: perisperm, **black pepper** or beet; endosperm, **castor**, wheat, maize or coconut.
